+## The portal (read-only pages)
+
+`/ui/` shows the lead book in a browser: a summary that adds up, the leads, and the items that need a person. The pages read the lead book through a second, read-only database connection (a write through it fails, and one test shows that); the buttons that retry or resolve an item call the same `/review` routes as everything else. Every request, including the pages, must come with a `Host` header that names this computer (or a name from `DESK_ALLOWED_HOSTS`), and a request from a page on another address (a different `Origin`, or `Sec-Fetch-Site` other than `same-origin`) is refused with 403 before anything is read. The exact routes are in `contract.md`.
+
+**What the Host and Origin check does and does not do.** It stops web pages in a browser: a page on another web site, and a web site that tricks the browser into talking to this computer under another name ("DNS rebinding"). It does NOT stop a person or program that can reach the desk's address and sends the right `Host` header by hand. There is no login. So keep the desk on `127.0.0.1` (the default). If a container (n8n in Docker, for example) must reach it, bind to the one address that only your container network reaches (the Docker bridge or host address), never to the wildcard address (the one made of four zeros). A bind to any other address than this computer's own exposes every lead (names, e-mail addresses, phone numbers, messages) to everyone who can connect, and the desk prints a warning at start-up when you do that.
+
+**Limits and shutdown of the desk process.**
+
+* Portal: `/api/ui` answers at most 8 requests at once; more get 503 `busy`. The count "refused at the door" on the Overview page (requests the Host and Origin check turned away; not the same as the `door` of a lead, `form` or `webhook`) is kept in memory and starts again at 0 when the desk restarts: read it as "since desk start (HH:MM)".
+* Threads: the desk serves each connection on its own thread. Writing a reply has a total limit of `REPLY_WRITE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (5 s): a client that does not read its reply loses the thread after that.
+* Open files: at start-up the desk checks that the open-file limit of this computer covers `MAX_HANDLER_THREADS` (4 files per thread plus 64 for everything else; contract.md lists what the 4 and the 64 are made of). It raises the soft limit to exactly what is needed when the hard limit is unlimited or at least that, and otherwise refuses to start (exit code 3). The message tells you either to lower `MAX_HANDLER_THREADS` to a number it names (only when a setting of 8 or more fits the limit in force) or, when no allowed setting fits, to raise the open-file limit (`ulimit -n`).
+* Exit codes: 1 the sender cannot be reached; 2 another desk holds the lock file or the port cannot be bound; 3 the open-file limit is too low; 4 the desk refuses a setting when it is built (an AI adapter whose `timeout_seconds` is above the stuck grace minus its margin; the message says so).
+* Stopping: Ctrl+C or SIGTERM stop the background job first and then give the lock file back. A second signal while that runs is ignored on purpose, so it cannot cut a step short. If the desk is still there after about 10 seconds, end it with SIGKILL (on Windows, end the process); the lock is dropped and the next start puts every unfinished follow-up back.
+* Two limits apply. (1) At most `MAX_CONNECTIONS` (256) connections can be in their reading phase (request line, headers, body) at one time; a connection stops counting when the desk starts writing its reply, so a thread that is still finishing a follow-up does not count here. (2) At most `MAX_HANDLER_THREADS` (1024) handler threads exist in total: a thread counts from the moment it gets a connection until it ends, so the reply write, the draining and the follow-up count too. When either limit is reached and nothing can be closed to make room, the new connection gets 503 `busy` and nothing is stored.
+* Deadlines: request line plus headers must arrive within `HEADER_READ_DEADLINE_SECONDS` and the body within `BODY_READ_DEADLINE_SECONDS` (10 s each, measured in real time from the start of that phase, however slowly the bytes trickle in); a stalled sender gets 408 `{"error":"request_timeout","receipt_id":null}`, and nothing was stored, so the same request id can be sent again.
+* Making room: when 256 connections are being read, the oldest one that is still being read (headers or body) and has sent no byte for `EVICT_MIN_IDLE_SECONDS` (2 s) is closed with no reply (its sender sees a dropped connection, nothing was stored, and it must send the request again; the same request id is safe). A connection that keeps sending is never closed this way. A connection is also safe from the moment its whole request has been read, parsed and checked (JSON and envelope) and the desk starts acting on it. When none can be closed, the new connection gets 503 `{"error":"busy","reason":"busy"}` with `Retry-After: 1`. That 503 is written before any receipt exists, and it is a different 503 from the ones for a database lock (`/leads`) and for more than 8 portal requests at once (`/api/ui`, body `{"error":"busy"}`).
+* Keep the desk off networks you do not trust. A bind written as a short all-zeros form (for example the single digit 0) is treated as a wildcard. Close the portal while you run a load test: every open page asks the desk for its numbers every few seconds.
