+Input rules (each has a test in `tests/test_hardening.py`): (1) JSON nested so deeply that the parser gives up is a 400 `bad_envelope` (counted in `rejected_envelopes`), not a 500; for `/resolve` it is a 400 `bad_decision`. (2) The stored copy of the payload escapes every non-ASCII character (`ensure_ascii`), so a JSON escape for half a character (a lone surrogate) is stored as text; a name, email, phone, company or message that contains one cannot be stored and gives verdict `review` with reason `bad_field:<field>`. (3) An email whose lower-case form is longer than 254 characters (some letters become two when lower-cased) gives verdict `review`, reason `invalid_email`, not a 500. (4) Only the thread that won the claim asks the AI step, the CRM or the sender, so two requests that drive the same new lead at the same moment, or a request and the reaper, ask the AI step once (the claim comes first; the reaper only drives `pending` rows, and a claimed row is `sending`); a CRM row can be put again only after a leftover intent mark, and then only on a keyed CRM, see the CRM row above. A failed AI step after the claim is one automatic try (`internal:<class>`; the row goes back to `pending` with a backoff through `_abort_unsent`, and to `failed` only when the cap is already reached; after a person's Retry the count starts at 1, so the row is `pending`). A lock on any write after the claim, the first stage stamp included, gives the try back instead (see the line on locks below). The model step is asked once PROVIDED the model answers within the stuck grace (send timeout + 4 busy waits, 25 s with the defaults): the desk cannot stop a call that runs, so a longer call is overtaken by the reaper and the model is asked a second time (the CRM row and the email stay once, every write checks the claim's token). `MODEL_CALL_BUDGET_SECONDS` (10 s) is the answer time the desk expects (a slower call is logged). The row is stamped again right after the model answers and before the label