 * `POST /fake/outbox` `{"key","to","subject","body"}` -> keyed mode: 200 `{"status":"sent","mode":"keyed"}` the first time for a key and 200 `{"status":"already_sent","mode":"keyed"}` after; blind mode: always 200 `{"status":"sent","mode":"blind"}` and every post is stored. Bad body: 400 `{"error":"bad_body"}`.
 * `GET /fake/outbox` -> `{"mode":"keyed|blind","count":n,"messages":[{"key","to","subject"}]}`.
 * `POST /fake/crm` `{"lead_id","record":{...}}` -> 200 `{"status":"put"|"already_present","mode":"..."}` (keyed by `lead_id`; for the n8n-only runs the CRM runs in blind counting mode, `--crm blind`: every post is stored). `GET /fake/crm` -> `{"count":n,"rows":[{"lead_id","record"}]}`.
-* `GET /fake/info` -> `{"outbox_capability":"keyed|blind","mode":"keyed|blind"}`.
+* `GET /fake/info` -> `{"outbox_capability":"keyed|blind","mode":"keyed|blind","crm_capability":"keyed|blind"}` (`crm_capability` is the mode of the fake CRM; it is set like the outbox mode: `--crm keyed|blind` on the command line, or the `crm_mode` argument of `FakesState`).
 * `POST /fake/control` `{"fail_next":"none|refuse|drop|record_then_drop|store_then_delay","delay_ms":int,"target":"outbox|crm"}` (`target` optional, default `outbox`; so that a slow CRM step and a slow mail step can both be reproduced; a `target` that is neither gives `bad_control`) -> 200 `{"status":"ok"}`; bad body 400 `{"error":"bad_control"}`. Applies to the next post to the TARGET only (outbox post or CRM post; the other target's control is untouched); the behaviours below are described for the outbox and apply the same to a CRM post (`refuse` 422 nothing stored, `drop` never arrived, `record_then_drop` stored and no reply, `store_then_delay`, `none` + `delay_ms` waits before storing): `refuse` answers 422 (nothing stored), `drop` closes the connection without recording ("never arrived"), `record_then_drop` stores the email and closes without replying ("sent, reply lost"), `store_then_delay` stores the email, waits `delay_ms`, then answers normally (the n8n test point called K2 in the README). With `fail_next:"none"`, `delay_ms` waits BEFORE storing and then answers normally (the n8n test point called K1 in the README, the timeout row of the error table, and `test_reply_does_not_wait_for_send`).
